WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.960
How do you learn languages when you're
not in the country where the language

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.120
is spoken or the languages are spoken?

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.700
I think that's the
situation for many of us.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.420
To speak well, usually
we need to speak a lot.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.630
That's been my experience in most cases.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:21.060
However, I'm gonna introduce you
to someone today who speaks a wide

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.920
variety of languages, remarkably well,
and she's learned them all at home.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.795
Iclal From Turkey.

00:00:27.884 --> 00:00:30.225
I think you'll find this very interesting.

00:00:30.525 --> 00:00:32.625
Hi, today I have a special guest.

00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:38.415
It's Iclal from Turkey, who is a
wonderful polyglot, a young polyglot.

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:39.105
Hi Iclal.

00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:39.345
Hi.

00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:44.835
So, uh, one, first of all, if you
enjoy my videos, please uh, subscribe.

00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:46.455
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:50.955
If you're following me on a uh, uh,
podcast service, please leave a comment.

00:00:51.285 --> 00:00:56.265
So I, I'm really happy to have
here for a number of reasons.

00:00:56.805 --> 00:00:59.245
First of all, because she
is a wonderful polyglot.

00:00:59.265 --> 00:01:02.235
We had a conversation on her
channel in different languages,

00:01:02.894 --> 00:01:04.645
uh, because she's from Istan...

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.425
I don't know her actually,
if she's from Istanbul.

00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:07.935
She will tell us.

00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:09.255
She's from Istan.

00:01:09.685 --> 00:01:13.575
And uh, and then I wanna talk a
little, because I haven't talked

00:01:13.575 --> 00:01:15.585
to that many polyglots from Turkey.

00:01:15.585 --> 00:01:16.395
Maybe there are a lot.

00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:17.295
I just haven't met them.

00:01:17.865 --> 00:01:21.195
And so I'm gonna, we're gonna have a
bit of a conversation about the role

00:01:21.195 --> 00:01:26.145
of geography in language learning,
but first I would like to ask Iclal to

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:30.765
sort of introduce herself, explain her
interest in different languages, why

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.345
she learned them in different languages.

00:01:33.435 --> 00:01:34.765
Can you do that for us Iclal?

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:35.475
I guess.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:38.820
Give it a try.

00:02:19.359 --> 00:03:46.419
So, uh...

00:04:20.706 --> 00:04:20.708
yeah.

00:04:20.708 --> 00:04:20.710
Um.

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:22.050
No, I'm just saying that again.

00:05:39.765 --> 00:05:40.535
Yeah, that was all.

00:05:41.035 --> 00:05:42.945
Wonderful, wonderful, wonderful.

00:05:43.545 --> 00:05:48.705
Um, you know, I, uh, I'm very
impressed with your ability

00:05:48.705 --> 00:05:49.875
to speak these languages.

00:05:49.905 --> 00:05:53.565
Uh, your pronunciation, your grammar.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.979
So, uh, before we get into this
theme of geography, I, I wanted to

00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:02.820
ask you, for example, I noticed that
your cases in Russian were very good.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.650
How did you go about learning...

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.950
and of course in German too, as
you said, both those languages

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.170
have somewhat complicated grammar.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.469
How did you go about learning the,
uh, grammar in those languages?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.680
So, I think I have a dis...

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.330
so I think I have a different perspective
of grammar because some people are

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.780
really, ignoring when it comes to grammar.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.400
They say that it's not useless.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.620
No, they say that it's not useful at
all, but actually I have another opinion.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.700
I find grammar pretty useful, and grammar
actually helped me a lot while I was

00:06:35.705 --> 00:06:38.400
learning those, uh, Italian and Spanish.

00:06:38.405 --> 00:06:42.870
But sometimes I realize that I pay
way too much attention to grammar.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.490
For example, in Russian,
that's what I did.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.100
I learned only grammar and nothing else.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.040
Actually, right now, my
grammar is nearly perfect.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:54.030
But, um, doing that actually
wasn't really a good choice.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:58.920
I, maybe I had to listen to more
Russian, I had to speak more, but

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.110
yeah, I know that my grammar is okay
because, um, there was a time where I

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.180
studied only grammar and nothing else.

00:07:06.185 --> 00:07:07.580
But I think that grammar is...

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.810
like you should pay attention to
grammar, but only at a necessary amount.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:17.490
And if you just exaggerate the attention
that you uh, give to grammar, then

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.600
everything becomes complicated as
what I experienced with Russian.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.510
I just, I just hate the
language because of the grammar.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.230
But for example, with German, I just
learn to grammar regularly without paying

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:32.820
too much attention to it, but at the
same time without ignoring the grammar.

00:07:32.825 --> 00:07:37.110
So I think that you should somehow just
keep the balance between grammar and

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:42.540
other skills, and that's how I just
manage speaking in a true way, let's say.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.130
Now the, I I thought about this today.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.490
You know, this, this
question of geography.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:54.600
So, uh, apparently in Ethiopia people
are, uh, multilingual because there's

00:07:54.605 --> 00:07:59.160
so many different languages in Ethiopia
or if you live in Luxembourg, you are

00:07:59.165 --> 00:08:00.960
surrounded by many different languages.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.340
Uh, if you live in the United States,
uh, or even in most of Canada,

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.390
you've got no one to speak to in any
other language other than English.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.440
Uh, for example, the Spaniards
are not as good as the Portuguese.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.180
The Portuguese are a smaller country.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.289
Uh, you know, Russia is a large country.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:22.710
I don't know how good they are
at speaking languages or Japan

00:08:22.715 --> 00:08:23.880
where I live for many years.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.640
Uh, I don't think the language
skills are that great.

00:08:26.969 --> 00:08:30.480
Uh, I don't know what the situation is in
Turkey, but to what extent do you think

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:36.059
that your location, physical location,
in today's day and age with the internet

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:42.000
and so forth, how much does that affect
your language learning possibilities.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.470
Yeah, good question.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:50.280
I think so actually I live in Turkey, and
Turkey is a really multicultural country

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.070
and it's becoming this every other day.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.700
Like we have a lot of tourists
and we have a lot of people

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:57.670
who start living in Turkey.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:05.055
And because of all these reasons, like
it's really easy to find an international,

00:09:05.085 --> 00:09:07.605
uh, uh, environment here in, in Turkey.

00:09:07.605 --> 00:09:11.955
Just if I, just specifically European
coast, I would just find a lot of

00:09:11.955 --> 00:09:15.855
tourists to talk to, especially
Russians, people who come from

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:18.105
Arabic countries, Spanish people.

00:09:18.765 --> 00:09:19.095
Yeah.

00:09:19.635 --> 00:09:23.625
I think right now we have a lot of
possibilities, but, uh, what I'd like to

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:30.075
mention is that I have a lot of polylglot
friends, which are particularly from

00:09:30.075 --> 00:09:34.005
Europe, they live in European countries
and uh, they're also polyglots like

00:09:34.005 --> 00:09:42.735
me, but I have a really different thing
that actually makes me, um, Kind of

00:09:43.245 --> 00:09:47.715
different than them because people are
just like, I was born in that country

00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:51.915
and my mother was actually bilingual,
and then I studied in that country.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.975
Then we moved to that country
so I learned that language too.

00:09:54.975 --> 00:09:59.805
And I was at Spain and all holidays I
went to Italy, I went to France, but

00:10:00.155 --> 00:10:03.045
I actually have been abroad only once.

00:10:03.345 --> 00:10:06.855
And that was a country that
I didn't speak the language.

00:10:07.395 --> 00:10:08.155
So I think...

00:10:08.155 --> 00:10:08.915
Which country was that?

00:10:08.955 --> 00:10:09.705
The Netherlands.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:14.790
Right now I'm learning the language,
but when I visited there, back

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.380
then, I didn't speak the language.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.330
So I have never been to France.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:19.620
I have never been to Russia.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.530
I don't know where, when I'm going
to actually visit these countries.

00:10:22.535 --> 00:10:23.520
That's another question.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:28.005
So I think that, uh, That's the
main thing that differentiates

00:10:28.005 --> 00:10:29.395
me from other polyglots.

00:10:29.415 --> 00:10:33.795
And maybe that's the thing that makes me
inspirational because people say that,

00:10:33.855 --> 00:10:37.755
okay, maybe it's possible to learn all
these languages when you stay in your own

00:10:37.755 --> 00:10:40.365
country and don't visit just overseas.

00:10:40.695 --> 00:10:45.285
But I think that if I was born in a
European country, maybe I would have more

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:49.695
opportunities to learn all these languages
and maybe that would be easier for me.

00:10:49.695 --> 00:10:51.885
But also in Turkey, I think that...

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.350
yeah, as long as you haven't
true that connection, I think

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:56.400
you can learn everything.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.690
The country doesn't play a big role
here, but the importance of Turkey

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:04.200
in my language learning is that,
is that we have a lot of tourists.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:08.005
I think like I can just easily
find native speakers or...

00:11:08.505 --> 00:11:12.915
I just meet other polyglots who come
to visit Turkey because we have a lot

00:11:12.915 --> 00:11:18.255
of tourist destinations and people just
want to, um, witness all this history.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.255
Do, do you spend a lot of time
speaking with these people?

00:11:21.495 --> 00:11:21.824
Yeah,

00:11:21.824 --> 00:11:25.724
actually when, whenever I just, uh,
visit the European coast because it's

00:11:25.724 --> 00:11:30.255
kind of a more tourist place, then the
place where I live right now, I live in

00:11:30.255 --> 00:11:36.930
Asia, so there are a lot of toursits and
they're most likely eager to help you.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.160
And actually...

00:11:38.339 --> 00:11:40.800
When you say European coast,
what are you referring to?

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.850
You not mean like İzmir or Antalya?

00:11:43.079 --> 00:11:43.439
No, no,

00:11:43.439 --> 00:11:43.620
no.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:44.760
Actually I don't mean that.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.550
Istanbul has, uh, two continents.

00:11:47.579 --> 00:11:48.540
Oh, the European

00:11:48.839 --> 00:11:50.264
side of Istanbul?

00:11:50.264 --> 00:11:50.584
Yes.

00:11:50.584 --> 00:11:57.449
So Istanbul has two sides and the European
one is kinda the place the tourists...

00:11:57.449 --> 00:11:58.319
More international.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:11:58.620
Yeah.

00:11:59.415 --> 00:12:02.475
So whenever I talk to tourists
they're just really kind.

00:12:02.475 --> 00:12:04.635
They're eager to help
me, especially Russians.

00:12:04.635 --> 00:12:08.655
I think they're just like, when they see
that you speak Russian, they are surprised

00:12:08.655 --> 00:12:09.975
and they just want to talk to you.

00:12:09.975 --> 00:12:12.705
They ask you a plenty amount of questions.

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:16.665
I think that Turks and Russians
just share this thing when we see a

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:18.825
foreigner who speaks our language.

00:12:18.825 --> 00:12:20.115
We're just amazed by that.

00:12:20.655 --> 00:12:22.305
I think that's really incredible.

00:12:22.955 --> 00:12:28.079
I can tell you, I can tell you
that the, uh, the, we have a lot

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:29.449
of Iranians here in Vancouver.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.839
If I speak to them in Persian that
they're just, they're just so happy.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.900
It's, they're the most positive
I have ever come across.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:41.010
We also have a lot of Iranian here
about, I don't speak their language.

00:12:42.209 --> 00:12:42.689
Right.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:43.500
Yeah.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.280
So in other words, what you're
saying is that geography, so it

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.910
helps in a way because you have a
lot of foreigners, visitors there.

00:12:51.495 --> 00:12:55.214
Uh, if you lived in Ankara, then
presumably it would be more difficult.

00:12:55.425 --> 00:12:57.375
Now how much do you talk on the internet?

00:12:58.515 --> 00:12:59.834
So actually I have...

00:12:59.834 --> 00:13:01.574
Or is it mostly people you meet in person?

00:13:02.295 --> 00:13:02.865
No, actually

00:13:02.865 --> 00:13:08.655
I use internet more because, uh, I can,
um, just find more people on internet

00:13:08.655 --> 00:13:12.314
and I have more options to talk to
because when you see someone, just when

00:13:12.314 --> 00:13:17.444
you prefer meeting someone to have a
language exchange with them, that can

00:13:17.444 --> 00:13:23.205
be sometimes real difficult because of
the scheduling, because of planning.

00:13:23.205 --> 00:13:23.835
I don't know.

00:13:23.835 --> 00:13:28.305
It's not really possible
to do that regularly and...

00:13:28.305 --> 00:13:28.575
yeah.

00:13:28.935 --> 00:13:33.105
But on the internet, actually,
we can talk to everyone, uh,

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.455
in each part of the world.

00:13:34.455 --> 00:13:36.525
So I prefer talking to people on internet.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:37.335
How do you do that?

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:39.915
Uh, is this language exchange
or who do you find to

00:13:39.915 --> 00:13:40.275
talk to?

00:13:40.855 --> 00:13:43.954
So sometimes I talk to my followers
because sometimes they say, hello

00:13:43.954 --> 00:13:47.145
Iclal, I'm from Russia and I'm
learning Turkish so if you like,

00:13:47.535 --> 00:13:51.314
Uh, I could help you in Russian and
you can just help me with Turkish.

00:13:51.314 --> 00:13:53.535
So I just accept that
and we become friends.

00:13:53.775 --> 00:13:57.734
Actually, some of my friends are
just, some of my friendships are

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.824
just like created like that from
my followers because we just have

00:14:01.844 --> 00:14:03.165
a language exchange with them.

00:14:03.464 --> 00:14:08.474
Or on the other side, I can also
name some, uh, language learning

00:14:08.474 --> 00:14:12.734
applications like Tandem, Hello
Talk also on these kind of things

00:14:12.734 --> 00:14:14.655
we can find other people to talk to.

00:14:15.015 --> 00:14:17.355
And there is a website that
I'd find really useful, which

00:14:17.355 --> 00:14:18.705
is Conversation Exchange.

00:14:18.705 --> 00:14:21.555
I have ha, I have found many
friends on this website.

00:14:21.855 --> 00:14:22.365
So...

00:14:22.755 --> 00:14:23.775
conversation exchange.

00:14:23.775 --> 00:14:24.465
Okay.

00:14:24.575 --> 00:14:28.275
Internet actually offers us
everything needed, and I think

00:14:28.275 --> 00:14:31.995
that everyone who wants to learn
languages must actually use that.

00:14:32.415 --> 00:14:32.635
But

00:14:34.005 --> 00:14:36.225
how do you get yourself to a
level where you can actually

00:14:36.225 --> 00:14:37.425
have a meaningful exchange?

00:14:37.425 --> 00:14:39.915
Say you start in a language from scratch.

00:14:39.915 --> 00:14:43.005
Now how are you gonna get to where you can
actually have an exchange with someone?

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:48.030
So actually I don't really prefer, uh,
talking to natives at the very beginning

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:49.800
because you don't have something to say.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:51.329
You just say, hello, how are you?

00:14:51.335 --> 00:14:53.250
My name is, I speak those languages.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.010
And you have the same
conversation with everyone else.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:59.970
And at this point, actually, you think
that you are having a proper conversation

00:14:59.975 --> 00:15:02.970
with someone, but actually what you're
doing is just repeating yourself and

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:04.710
you're, you're not learning something new.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.980
You're just learning the greetings, but
you know, already learned them before.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:12.465
So what I think is that, We should
start speaking to native speakers

00:15:12.465 --> 00:15:14.625
when we are at A2 or B1 level.

00:15:15.045 --> 00:15:19.185
For example, I have met a Russian
friend last year, and at the beginning I

00:15:19.185 --> 00:15:23.385
couldn't really have a conversation with
him because my Russian was really poor.

00:15:23.685 --> 00:15:26.355
But after he said that,
just tell me everything.

00:15:26.355 --> 00:15:30.035
You can just form a sentence
using the words you know.

00:15:30.064 --> 00:15:34.545
And after a month, I actually was at the
level of having a proper conversation

00:15:34.545 --> 00:15:36.055
with him, and that's amazing, I think.

00:15:36.365 --> 00:15:42.180
So we should just wait until A2 or
B1 and then we should do our best.

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:42.450
So while

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:45.180
you're waiting, what do you do
then in if with these languages?

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.520
Say you're waiting, you're not
yet, you're waiting to, how do

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.300
you get yourself to the A2 B1

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:49.560
level?

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.230
So actually I'm just grabbing the
basics because if you don't have the

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.800
basics in that language, it's, it's
actually kind of difficult to speak.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:59.401
For example, imagine that you're speaking
Italian and if you don't know past tense,

00:15:59.406 --> 00:16:03.060
if you don't know future tense, if you
don't know the personal pronouns, actually

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.560
it's impossible to speak to someone.

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:09.615
So once you get the basics from a
material from a particular thing uh,

00:16:09.615 --> 00:16:13.605
you say, okay, I have completed A1, I
have completed A2, and then right now

00:16:13.605 --> 00:16:15.194
I can start talking to native speakers.

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:21.105
So at that level, when I feel that
I can construct a few, um, a, that

00:16:21.105 --> 00:16:26.505
field was driven a few phrases
in languages, so then I start for

00:16:26.505 --> 00:16:28.005
native speakers and I talk to them.

00:16:28.725 --> 00:16:28.845
Mm-hmm.

00:16:29.655 --> 00:16:29.925
Yeah.

00:16:31.455 --> 00:16:34.155
Well, I think your example
is, uh, is very encouraging.

00:16:34.815 --> 00:16:39.405
Uh, for someone who is, uh, not in
the country where the language is

00:16:39.405 --> 00:16:43.155
spoken, and yet you've achieved a very
high level in, in those languages.

00:16:43.785 --> 00:16:47.265
And, uh, I think that's a good
example for, for people who

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.255
are interested in language.

00:16:48.255 --> 00:16:48.585
I hope.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:49.770
People...

00:16:50.099 --> 00:16:50.490
yeah.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:55.140
So, and, and, uh, especially at such an
a young age to speak so many languages

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.370
so fluently, I'm most impressed.

00:16:56.579 --> 00:16:57.599
So thank you very much...

